---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const fall = assertion('the-class-got-smaller-as-the-county-emptied')
const per = assertion('every-adult-but-the-librarian')
const lib = assertion('eleven-districts-with-no-librarian')
const adm = assertion('the-administrators-that-did-not-leave')
const aides = assertion('one-aide-for-every-hundred-children')
const psych = assertion('the-psychologists-belong-to-the-service-center')
const bldg = assertion('eight-buildings-and-seven-were-limas')
const conv = assertion('two-answers-that-arrive-at-the-same-building')

const spec = (a: typeof fall, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const perSpec = spec(per, 'Full-time equivalents per 1,000 pupils')
const libSpec = spec(lib, 'Full-time equivalents, all twelve districts')
const bldgSpec = spec(bldg, 'School buildings')
---

<Article slug="the-adults-in-the-building">
  <p>
    This county's record of its schools has been a record of children and money: how many were
    enrolled, how they scored, what a district spent on each of them and where the money came from.
    The people the money buys were missing. They are in the same federal file as the enrolment, one
    column over, for every year since 1992 — teachers, aides, counsellors, librarians,
    administrators, and a count of the buildings going back to 1986.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={fall} open />
  <p>
    Which is the first surprise. A district losing a third of its children over thirty-two years
    might be expected to shed teachers at the same rate, and Allen County's shed them at two thirds
    of it. The class is smaller than it was, in every district and in the county as a whole.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={per} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="per"
    title="Adults per thousand children"
    caption="Full-time equivalents per 1,000 pupils across the twelve districts holding ground in Allen County, 1992 against 2024. Four categories rose against a falling denominator and one fell against it."
    columns={['Category', 'Per 1,000 pupils']}
    rows={per.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(2)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="per" set:html={JSON.stringify(perSpec)} is:inline />

  <h2>The room stayed open</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={lib} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="lib"
    title="The library and the librarian"
    caption="Certified library specialists and library support staff, all twelve districts. The certified post went and the assistant stayed, which is why this is a loss rather than a relabelling."
    columns={['Column', 'Full-time equivalents']}
    rows={lib.figures.map((f) => [f.label, String(f.value)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="lib" set:html={JSON.stringify(libSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Twenty-four certified librarians served twelve districts in 1992, at least one in every one of
    them. The whole county now reports four, all four of them in Lima. Nothing else these schools
    employ moved like that.
  </p>
  <Gloss>
    A steep fall in a single column of an administrative file is the most publishable thing in it
    and the least reliable. The check is on the form: sum the column with the ones the agency printed
    beside it, because that is where a reclassified job goes. Library support staff read 12 in 1992
    and 11 now, so the librarians are gone. Run the same test on the administrators, whose lead
    column falls by two thirds in a single year, and the four columns together barely move. Nothing
    was cut there. A line on a form was.
  </Gloss>
  <AssertionCard assertion={adm} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={aides} />
  <p>
    An aide is not a cheaper teacher and the file will not say what any of them does. What it does
    say is that the adult a child in this county is most likely to have met who was not a teacher in
    1992 was an administrator, and is now an aide.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={psych} />

  <h2>Eight buildings</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={bldg} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="bldg"
    title="Fifty-one schoolhouses, and forty-three"
    caption="School buildings reported by the twelve districts. Lima City accounts for seven of the eight that closed; five districts have reported the same number every year since 1986."
    columns={['Count', 'Buildings']}
    rows={bldg.figures.map((f) => [f.label, String(f.value)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="bldg" set:html={JSON.stringify(bldgSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={conv} />
  <p>
    Two districts facing the same arithmetic answered it in opposite ways and ended in the same
    place. Lima closed schools until the ones left were full; the rest of the county kept its
    buildings and let them empty. Both now hold about 370 children to a building, which is the sort
    of agreement that looks like a plan and is not one.
  </p>
</Article>
